sections.s2_technical:
Consensus is the problem of getting a set of unreliable machines to agree on a value or a sequence of values despite crashes and network delays. It is the foundation for anything that needs a single source of truth across many nodes — cluster membership, configuration, leader locks, metadata. Raft was designed to be understandable, decomposing the problem into leader election, log replication, and safety, with a strong-leader model where all changes flow through one leader at a time.

Leader election. Raft divides time into terms, each identified by a monotonically increasing number. Every node is in one of three states: follower, candidate, or leader. Followers expect periodic heartbeats from the leader. If a follower hears nothing for a randomised election timeout, it suspects the leader is gone, increments the term, becomes a candidate, votes for itself, and sends RequestVote messages to the others. A node grants its vote to at most one candidate per term, and only to a candidate whose log is at least as up to date as its own. A candidate that collects votes from a majority becomes the leader and starts sending heartbeats. The randomised timeouts make split votes rare and self-correcting, so the cluster converges on exactly one leader per term.

```java
// Election (conceptual): a candidate needs a MAJORITY of votes to lead
int votes = 1; // votes for itself
for (Node peer : peers) if (peer.requestVote(term, lastLogIndex, lastLogTerm)) votes++;
boolean becomesLeader = votes > clusterSize / 2;  // strict majority (quorum)
```

Log replication. Once elected, the leader is the sole entry point for client commands. It appends each command to its own log as a new entry tagged with the current term, then sends AppendEntries messages to replicate the entry to followers. When a majority of nodes — including the leader — have stored the entry, the leader marks it committed and applies it to its state machine, then tells followers they may apply it too. Because every node applies committed entries in the same order, all state machines stay identical. AppendEntries also serves as the heartbeat and carries consistency checks so a follower whose log has diverged is repaired by the leader overwriting the conflicting suffix with the leader's entries.

```java
// Replication (conceptual): commit when a MAJORITY have stored the entry
log.append(new Entry(term, command));
int stored = 1;
for (Node follower : peers) if (follower.appendEntries(term, entry)) stored++;
if (stored > clusterSize / 2) { commitIndex = entry.index; applyToStateMachine(entry); }
```

Safety. The subtle part is guaranteeing that a committed entry is never lost even when leaders change. Raft enforces several rules: a node votes for a candidate only if the candidate's log is at least as up to date, so a node missing committed entries can never become leader; a leader only considers an entry from its own term committed once it is on a majority, which prevents older entries from being resurrected or overwritten incorrectly; and a new leader never deletes or overwrites committed entries, only brings followers into line with them. Together these mean that once an entry is committed, every future leader has it, and all nodes converge on the same log forever.

A critical property is the quorum requirement: commitment needs a strict majority, so a cluster of five tolerates two failures, a cluster of three tolerates one. This is why Raft clusters are sized at odd numbers — three or five — to maximise fault tolerance per node while keeping a clear majority. If a partition leaves the leader in a minority, it cannot commit new entries (no majority), and the majority side elects a new leader, so there is never a scenario where two partitions both commit conflicting entries; the minority simply stalls until it rejoins.

In practice Raft powers the control planes of many systems. etcd uses Raft to store Kubernetes cluster state consistently, so every control-plane component reads the same truth. Consul uses Raft to keep its service catalog and key-value store consistent across servers for service discovery and configuration. Kafka's KRaft mode uses a Raft-based metadata log to replace ZooKeeper, so brokers agree on topic, partition, and leadership metadata without a separate coordination system. The senior-level nuance is that Raft is used for the metadata and coordination plane, which is small, critical, and requires strong consistency, not for the bulk high-throughput data plane; a bank would use Raft to agree on which node is the primary or on configuration, while the actual transaction throughput is handled by the data store that Raft coordinates. Understanding this boundary — strong consensus for the small critical state, scalable replication for the large data — is what separates a surface knowledge of Raft from knowing where it belongs.

